{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-270776",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}-10\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{y}-10.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{y}-10 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{y} & =x+10 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+10) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2}(x+10))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{4}(x+10)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{4}(x+10)^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0,\\,f(x)\\ge -10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge -10,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 0.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
